A||||6||ChadMan||What's up with my oil pressure?||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||05-29-2001||07:10 PM||||My 88 Coupe has always made good oil pressure (with warm engine, 25 at idle, 45-50 cruising).  Last week, the oil pressure gage went haywire, bouncing all over hell and eventually landing on zero (!).  <P>I searched the Forum (bless you all) and decided I had a bad oil pressure sending unit.  I bought a new one at NAPA today and installed it - the new unit is smaller, but the threads and plug are the same.  Now the needle is steady, but the pressure reads about half what it should (just out of red at idle, 25 or so cruising).<P>So what do you guys think?  Is the new sending unit farqed up?  Is the gage itself bad?  Or is my Duke getting ready to die of oil starvation?<P>Thanks...||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||05-29-2001||07:13 PM||||Chad, have you checked the oil pressure manually by hooking a pressure gauge to the line (where the sending unit mounts)? my guess would be that it is a bad gauge as I had the same problem -- I knew the sending unit was good but the gauge read like yours -- changes the gauge and all is well.......but, by checking the pressure manually I knew that I had good oil pressure thus eliminating the pump as the problem....||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||05-29-2001||07:40 PM||||That might be correct. If there are any miles on the engine, 20PSI at idle hot is normal. GM acutally considers 10 PSI to be ok and 10PSI/1000 RPM.<P>Since the senders are lousy, the "higher" pressure you had might have been the sender error.<P>Again, if you don't get a light and have 20 PSI at idle and can hit 40-50 PSI wound up, it's fine.<P>If the engine is starving for oil, the lifters will start to clatter just before the crank and main bearings become "one".<P>From what you've described, you're fine.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||05-29-2001||11:22 PM||||Sending unit is prolly NFG<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/sig.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000004||Nikotek||05-30-2001||01:32 AM||||Chad,I've had the same problem with my 88 coupe.Here's what I found...I initially thought because of high mileage(178k)the engine wasn't holding pressure.I went through 2 units with low innacurate readings both times,until my local Napa parts guy found 2 different units(1 smaller than stock sender and 1 larger unit,same electrical connector).Put the larger sender in and problem solved...Why there are 2 different ones for the gauge and light versions is beyond me!Leads me to wonder what the smaller one is actually for?<P>------------------<BR>86' SE(308 kit)<BR>88' Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||05-30-2001||01:47 AM||||There are various versions of three senders. <P>60, 80, and 120 PSI. After 85 most were 80 PSI. After 92 some are 120 PSI. I figure it out the hard way. Why 120 PSI, I don't know.<P>The 88 sender is the same body as the later 120's. Maybe they gave you the wrong one. <P>I agree, maybe a remote gauge on it for a test is in order.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||05-30-2001||08:35 AM||||Thanks a lot, you guys.  I was 90% confident it was NOT an engine problem before...I am now 100% confident.  Nikotek, thanks for the tip on the two senders...I'll have to pull this NAPA one out and take it and the original one to NAPA and try to find the correct one.||||reg||6
